\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
Would mathematically capable non-human intelligences discover the same mathematics? This question becomes tractable after separating invariance under extension, invariance across models, invariance under structural recoding, and dependence on the choice of operation. We develop a general theory of universal consequences using a Tarskian closure operator. For a consistent base theory, the intersection of the consequence sets of all consistent extensions is exactly the consequence set of the base itself. In a semantic setting, extension-universality over a consistent theory is equivalent to ordinary semantic consequence; hence the assertion that one orientation of a sentence is universal is exactly a semantic decision assertion. A two-model criterion yields nonuniversality in both directions. We illustrate it with two finite incidence geometries, one satisfying and one refuting Playfair's parallel postulate. Turning to arithmetic, we show that prime elements are invariant under multiplicative equivalence, so every multiplicative recoding of the natural numbers contains images of arbitrarily large ordinary primes. In contrast, when tropical multiplication on natural numbers is ordinary addition, the unique nonzero irreducible is $1$. These results distinguish truths forced by a chosen base, truths preserved by structural translation, and truths altered by changing the primitive operations.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

Discussions of universal mathematics often conflate several claims. One says that a theorem remains valid when more axioms are added. Another says that every admissible mathematical world satisfies a sentence. A third says that a property survives translation between equivalent structures. A fourth says that a familiar concept such as primality should persist when the governing operation changes. These assertions require different definitions and have different truth conditions.

This paper supplies a common framework for separating them. Its objects are consequence operations, semantic theories, finite incidence geometries, multiplicative equivalences, and tropical addition. The question is structural rather than psychological: what facts are forced after assumptions and operations have been fixed?

Four conclusions organize the discussion. First, for a consistent base $B$, the statements shared by all consistent extensions are exactly the consequences of $B$. Second, extension-universality over a consistent semantic theory is ordinary semantic consequence; consequently, saying that one orientation of a sentence is universal is a decision claim, not a gift of consistency. Third, two explicit finite incidence worlds make Playfair's postulate true and false, respectively. Fourth, multiplicative equivalences preserve prime elements, whereas replacing multiplication by tropical multiplication changes the natural-number irreducibles from infinitely many primes to the sole element $1$.

\section{Consequence Operations and Universal Cores}

\begin{definition}
Let $S$ be a set of statements. A \emph{Tarskian consequence operation} is a map
\[
 C:\Pow(S)\longrightarrow\Pow(S)
\]
such that, for all $\Gamma,\Delta\subseteq S$,
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\roman*)]
\item $\Gamma\subseteq C(\Gamma)$ (extensivity);
\item $\Gamma\subseteq\Delta$ implies $C(\Gamma)\subseteq C(\Delta)$ (monotonicity);
\item $C(C(\Gamma))=C(\Gamma)$ (idempotence).
\end{enumerate}
A theory $\Gamma$ is \emph{consistent} when $C(\Gamma)\ne S$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}
For a base theory $B$, its \emph{universal core} is
\[
U_C(B)=\{\varphi\in S:\forall\Delta\supseteq B,
\ C(\Delta)\ne S\Longrightarrow\varphi\in C(\Delta)\}.
\]
Thus $U_C(B)$ is the intersection of the consequence sets of all consistent extensions of $B$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Persistence of consequences]
If $B\subseteq\Delta$, then $C(B)\subseteq C(\Delta)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
This is the monotonicity axiom. Every consequence available from $B$ remains available when the assumptions in $\Delta\setminus B$ are added.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Base consequences are universal]
For every base $B$, one has $C(B)\subseteq U_C(B)$.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
Let $\varphi\in C(B)$ and let $\Delta$ be a consistent extension of $B$. Persistence gives $\varphi\in C(\Delta)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Universal Core Theorem]
If $B$ is consistent, then
\[
U_C(B)=C(B).
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The preceding corollary gives $C(B)\subseteq U_C(B)$. Conversely, $B$ itself is a consistent extension of $B$. Every statement shared by all consistent extensions must therefore belong to $C(B)$.
\end{proof}

This theorem states exactly what base-relative universality provides. If Peano arithmetic is selected as a base, each theorem of that arithmetic survives in every consistent extension retaining its axioms. It does not follow that every intelligence must select this base, use the same language, or regard the base as complete.

\begin{theorem}[Downward preservation of consistency]
If $B\subseteq\Delta$ and $\Delta$ is consistent, then $B$ is consistent.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
If $C(B)=S$, monotonicity gives $S=C(B)\subseteq C(\Delta)$ and hence $C(\Delta)=S$, contrary to consistency.
\end{proof}

\section{Semantic Universality and Independence}

\begin{definition}
Let $W$ be a class of mathematical worlds. A \emph{sentence} is a property $\varphi:W\to\{\mathrm{true},\mathrm{false}\}$, and a semantic theory $T$ is a set of sentences. A world $w$ is a \emph{model} of $T$, written $w\modelsT T$, if every member of $T$ is true at $w$. The theory is \emph{semantically consistent} if it has a model. Semantic entailment is defined by
\[
T\modelsT\varphi\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad
\forall w,\ w\modelsT T\Longrightarrow\varphi(w).
\]
The sentence $\varphi$ is \emph{extension-universal over $T$} if every semantically consistent extension $V\supseteq T$ entails $\varphi$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Countermodel Principle]
If $w\modelsT T$ and $\varphi(w)$ is false, then $\varphi$ is not extension-universal over $T$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Let $V=T\cup\{\neg\varphi\}$. The world $w$ models $V$, so $V$ is consistent. It does not entail $\varphi$, because $w$ is a countermodel.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Two-Model Independence Principle]
Suppose $w_+$ and $w_-$ model $T$, with $\varphi(w_+)$ true and $\varphi(w_-)$ false. Then neither $\varphi$ nor $\neg\varphi$ is extension-universal over $T$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Apply the Countermodel Principle to $w_-$ and $\varphi$, then to $w_+$ and $\neg\varphi$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Extension-Universality Equivalence]
If $T$ is semantically consistent, then
\[
\varphi\text{ is extension-universal over }T
\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad T\modelsT\varphi.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
For the forward implication, take the consistent extension to be $T$ itself. Conversely, suppose $T\modelsT\varphi$ and $V\supseteq T$. Every model of $V$ is a model of $T$ and therefore satisfies $\varphi$; hence $V\modelsT\varphi$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Decision Equivalence]
For semantically consistent $T$,
\begin{align*}
&\bigl(\varphi\text{ is extension-universal over }T\bigr)
\lor\bigl(\neg\varphi\text{ is extension-universal over }T\bigr)\\
&\hspace{2cm}\Longleftrightarrow
(T\modelsT\varphi)\lor(T\modelsT\neg\varphi).
\end{align*}
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
Apply the Extension-Universality Equivalence separately to $\varphi$ and $\neg\varphi$.
\end{proof}

If $\mathrm{RH}$ denotes a sentence expressing the Riemann Hypothesis, the assertion that either $\mathrm{RH}$ or $\neg\mathrm{RH}$ is extension-universal over a consistent arithmetic base $T$ is exactly the assertion that $T$ semantically decides $\mathrm{RH}$. Consistency alone establishes neither orientation.

\section{A Finite Incidence Experiment}

Consider two worlds in which both points and lines are the set $\{0,1,2\}$. In the \emph{affine witness}, define incidence by
\[
p\,I_A\,\ell\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad p=\ell.
\]
In the \emph{intersecting witness}, define
\[
p\,I_I\,\ell\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad p=0\text{ or }p=\ell.
\]
Thus every line of the intersecting witness contains the common point $0$.

In either world, lines $\ell$ and $m$ are \emph{parallel} if they have no common incident point. Playfair's postulate states that for every line $\ell$ and every point $p$ not incident with $\ell$, there exists exactly one line $m$ through $p$ parallel to $\ell$.

\begin{theorem}[Affine witness satisfies Playfair]
Playfair's postulate holds in the affine witness.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Let $p$ be external to $\ell$. Incidence is equality, so $p\ne\ell$. The line $m=p$ contains $p$. Its sole point is $p$, whereas the sole point of $\ell$ is $\ell$, so the lines are parallel. Any line containing $p$ must equal $p$, proving uniqueness.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Intersecting witness refutes Playfair]
Playfair's postulate fails in the intersecting witness.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Every line contains point $0$, so no two lines are parallel. External pairs exist: point $1$ is not incident with line $2$. No parallel through that point can therefore exist.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Finite Parallel Independence]
Relative to the empty background theory of these incidence worlds, neither Playfair's postulate nor its negation is extension-universal.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The affine witness models the empty theory and satisfies Playfair, while the intersecting witness models the empty theory and refutes it. The Two-Model Independence Principle applies.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
The scope is deliberate. These finite witnesses isolate independence over an empty base. They are not asserted to satisfy a common neutral-geometry axiom system, and therefore are not substitutes for full Euclidean and hyperbolic models.
\end{remark}

\section{Structural Invariance of Prime Elements}

Let $M$ and $N$ be commutative monoids with zero. A \emph{multiplicative equivalence} is a bijection $e:M\to N$ that preserves multiplication, the identity, and the zero structure, and whose inverse has the same compatibility. An element $x$ is \emph{prime} if it is nonzero, is not a unit, and
\[
x\mid ab\quad\Longrightarrow\quad x\mid a\text{ or }x\mid b.
\]

\begin{theorem}[Prime Invariance]
If $e:M\to N$ is a multiplicative equivalence, then, for every $x\in M$,
\[
e(x)\text{ is prime in }N
\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad
x\text{ is prime in }M.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The equivalence preserves and reflects zero, units, products, and divisibility. In particular, $x\mid y$ if and only if $e(x)\mid e(y)$. Transporting each clause in the definition of primality across $e$ proves both implications.
\end{proof}

This theorem makes precise the obligations of an alien encoding. Once a translation preserves the multiplication-and-divisibility structure, it cannot erase primality.

\begin{theorem}[Unbounded Alien Primes]
Let $e$ be any multiplicative equivalence from the natural numbers to themselves. For every natural-number bound $B$, there is a natural number $p$ such that $p>B$, $p$ is prime, and $e(p)$ is prime in the encoded multiplication.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The infinitude of primes supplies a prime $p>B$. Prime Invariance then shows that $e(p)$ is prime.
\end{proof}

The order bound concerns the source number $p$: a purely multiplicative equivalence need not preserve order. What is invariant is the unbounded supply of source primes whose images remain prime.

\section{Tropical Irreducibility}

In min-plus tropical arithmetic, tropical multiplication on natural numbers is ordinary addition:
\[
a\odot b=a+b.
\]
The tropical multiplicative identity is therefore ordinary $0$.

\begin{definition}
A natural number $n$ is \emph{tropically irreducible} when $n\ne0$ and every expression $n=a\odot b$, equivalently $n=a+b$, satisfies $a=0$ or $b=0$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Classification of Tropical Irreducibles]
For every natural number $n$,
\[
n\text{ is tropically irreducible}
\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad n=1.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The number $1$ is nonzero, and if $1=a+b$ for natural numbers, one summand must be $0$. Thus $1$ is irreducible. Conversely, if $n$ is nonzero and not $1$, then $n\ge2$ and
\[
n=1+(n-1)
\]
is a decomposition into two nonzero natural numbers. Hence $n$ is reducible.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Uniqueness]
Any two tropically irreducible natural numbers are equal.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
Each equals $1$ by the classification theorem.
\end{proof}

Ordinary multiplication has infinitely many prime natural numbers; tropical multiplication on the same underlying set has exactly one nonzero irreducible. The operation defining factorization, not merely the notation, has changed.

\section{Algorithms and Numerical Experiments}

The incidence results admit exhaustive finite computation. Given $P$ points, $L$ lines, and an incidence table, enumerate each external point--line pair. For each pair, scan all candidate lines through the point and all points that might witness an intersection with the reference line. Playfair's condition holds exactly when every candidate set has cardinality one. A direct implementation takes $O(P^2L^2)$ time and $O(L)$ auxiliary space apart from the incidence table.

Tropical irreducibility can be tested by enumerating $a=1,\ldots,n-1$ and checking the decomposition $n=a+(n-a)$. This takes $O(n)$ time and $O(1)$ space. The classification theorem improves the decision procedure to constant time: return true exactly when $n=1$.

To exhibit an ordinary prime above a bound $B$, one may test successive integers using trial division through their square roots. If the first prime found is $p$, this uses $O((p-B)\sqrt p)$ elementary divisibility tests in the worst case. A finite permutation of labels can illustrate representational change, while Prime Invariance supplies the general structural conclusion.

\section{Applications and Interpretation}

Prime-number signals in interstellar communication are compelling because primality is structural under multiplication. Sender and recipient need not share decimal notation. If they identify corresponding multiplication structures, prime patterns survive. This does not guarantee that the recipient regards multiplication as fundamental or immediately recognizes the encoding.

The results suggest a hierarchy. \emph{Deductive persistence} concerns conclusions under added assumptions. \emph{Semantic necessity} concerns truth in all models of a base. \emph{Structural invariance} concerns properties preserved by equivalences respecting specified operations. \emph{Operational dependence} concerns the change in concepts when primitive operations themselves change. Broad claims of universality are ambiguous until their level is identified.

The Riemann Hypothesis is an instructive test. Any claim that every sufficiently rich arithmetic worldview proves it or its negation requires a specified base, language, semantics, and meaning of richness. Once those are fixed, the Decision Equivalence shows that the universality claim is exactly a semantic decision claim.

\section{Comparative Synthesis}

The examples form a diagnostic procedure for claims about mathematical inevitability. First specify a base theory. If a proposed truth is merely claimed to survive stronger assumptions, monotonicity settles the issue. If it is claimed to hold in every admissible world, search for models on opposite sides. If it is claimed to survive translation, list the operations and distinguished elements preserved by the translation. If an operation itself changes, rebuild the relevant definition before comparing outcomes.

This procedure prevents two mistakes. An extension retains all base assumptions, whereas a different model may change an undecided sentence. Likewise, tropicalization is not a renaming: replacing ordinary multiplication by ordinary addition changes factorization itself. Prime Invariance and the classification of tropical irreducibles therefore complement rather than contradict each other.

The framework gives a restrained notion of shared discovery. Two intelligences can discover corresponding prime elements despite different notation, provided their arithmetic systems are multiplicatively equivalent. They need not agree about a geometric sentence when their accepted models differ. Agreement is compelled only after the comparison map, assumptions, and semantics are explicit.

\section{Limitations and Future Work}

The consequence operation is abstract, and no particular first-order calculus is developed here. Connecting syntactic and semantic consequence requires soundness and completeness for a chosen language. The finite incidence witnesses establish independence only over an empty base, not over neutral geometry. Prime Invariance assumes a full multiplicative equivalence; weaker translations may preserve only part of the factorization structure. Finally, the tropical classification concerns natural numbers, not tropical polynomials, whose factorization theory is substantially richer.

Natural next steps are to instantiate the consequence operation with first-order Peano arithmetic; construct Euclidean and hyperbolic models sharing neutral axioms; state the Riemann Hypothesis in an arithmetic language while separating standard truth, semantic decision, syntactic decision, and independence; characterize factorization properties preserved by weaker translations; and develop the theory of tropical-polynomial irreducibility.

\section{Conclusion}

Universal mathematics is layered. Relative to a consistent base, the universal core is exactly what the base already entails. Across models, positive and negative witnesses destroy universality in both directions. Across multiplicatively equivalent encodings, primality survives. Across a change from ordinary to tropical multiplication, irreducibility changes dramatically, leaving only $1$ among natural numbers.

Equivalent structures compel equivalent structural discoveries; different axioms permit different worlds; different operations generate different arithmetic atoms. What another intelligence must discover depends on the bridge by which its mathematics is compared with ours.

